    "https://uofl.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/UofLCareerSite/job/Belknap-Campus/Part-Time-Lecturer--Jazz-Trumpet_R109505": {
      "title": "Part-Time Lecturer, Jazz Trumpet",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-10-13\nPart time\n\nDepartment: Location: Belknap Campus Time Type: Part time Worker Type: Temporary (Fixed Term) Job Req ID: R109505 Position Description: The University of Louisville School of Music seeks an outstanding candidate to serve as Lecturer in Jazz Trumpet beginning January 1, 2027. This is a five-month, part-time faculty position that will hold the rank of part-time lecturer (PTL). Core duties will include teaching applied jazz trumpet. Applied lesson compensation assumes involvement in jazz area meetings, end-of-semester area juries, auditions, and other matters pertaining to the applied area. This is a non-tenurable appointment and does not include health benefits. Qualifications Candidates should have professional-level performance ability and successful private teaching experience. A minimum of a Bachelor of Music, plus professional experience, is required. Application Procedures Application materials should include: Cover letter describing qualifications for the position Curriculum vitae or resume in PDF format Complete contact information, including address, phone number, and email address, for three professional references Additional materials may be requested at a later date, including links to performance videos. For full consideration, please submit application materials by the stated deadline. About the City and the University of Louisville School of Music Louisville is a progressive community of approximately 620,000 people with a robust economy, a vibrant arts scene, a nationally ranked public school system, and a rich history infused with southern hospitality. Situated on the banks of the Ohio River, Kentucky’s largest city boasts a cost of living substantially lower than the national average, with a broad range of affordable housing options from high-rise apartments and historic Victorian homes to suburban communities and rural farms. The University of Louisville is Kentucky’s premier, nationally recognized metropolitan research university. The School of Music, a fully accredited member of NASM since 1938, is a member of the Kentucky state system of higher education. UofL has experienced steady growth, with a current enrollment of 23,000 students, 360 of whom are enrolled in the School of Music in undergraduate and graduate degree programs. Equal Employment Opportunity The University of Louisville is an Equal Employment Opportunity employer. The University strives to provide equal employment opportunity on the basis of merit and without unlawful discrimination on the basis of race, sex, age, color, national origin, ethnicity, creed, religion, disability, genetic information, sexual orientation, gender, gender identity or expression, veteran status, marital status, or pregnancy. In accordance with the Rehabilitation Act of 1973 and the Vietnam Era Veteran Readjustment Act of 1974, the University prohibits job discrimination of individuals with disabilities, Vietnam era veterans, qualified special disabled veterans, recently separated veterans, and other protected veterans. The University acknowledges its obligations to ensure affirmative steps are taken to ensure equal employment opportunities for all employees and applicants for employment. It is the policy of the University that no employee or applicant for employment be subject to unlawful discrimination in terms of recruitment, hiring, promotion, contract, contract renewal, tenure, compensation, benefits, and/or working conditions. No employee or applicant for employment is required to endorse or condemn a specific ideology, political viewpoint, or social viewpoint to be eligible for hiring, contract renewal, tenure, or promotion. Consistent with applicable law, demographic information is collected for aggregate reporting requirements. Demographic information provided through this application is not available to hiring managers/committees and is not considered in hiring or employment decisions. Assistance and Accommodations Computers are available for application submission at the Human Resources Department located at 2315 South First Street Walk, Room 02C - Louisville, Kentucky 40292. If you require assistance or accommodation with our online application process, please contact us by email at  employment@louisville.edu  or by phone 502-852-6258."
    },
    "https://uofl.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/UofLCareerSite/job/Health-Sciences-Center/Research-Scientist_R109510": {
      "title": "Research Scientist",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-10-13\nFull time\n\nDepartment: Location: Health Sciences Center Time Type: Full time Worker Type: Regular Job Req ID: R109510 Minimum Requirements: Master's degree in a related field and four (4) years of relevant experience or an equivalent combination of education and experience. Grade 7 (Salaried) Position Description: The Department of Microbiology and Immunology is seeking a highly motivated and skilled Research Scientist to join the Dibra Lab, a highly collaborative, innovation-driven research group focused on understanding how tumors evade the immune system and identifying new therapeutic strategies to overcome immune resistance. Our work spans the full spectrum from discovery to functional validation, integrating cutting-edge genetic screens, spatial multi-omics, and mechanistic in vivo studies. We leverage advanced in vivo genetic screening platforms, including Perturb-map, with spatially resolved perturbations to define tumor–immune interactions at high resolution. Using single-cell and spatial transcriptomic technologies, including Xenium, we investigate cellular heterogeneity, immune niche architecture, and tumor lineage states within the tumor microenvironment. A central focus of the laboratory is understanding how oncogenic pathways reprogram both innate and adaptive immunity to promote immune evasion. We integrate these approaches with immunopeptidomics to define tumor-specific antigen-presentation programs and uncover new therapeutic vulnerabilities. These studies are supported by genetically engineered mouse models, novel alleles developed in the laboratory, and patient-derived organoid systems, providing an integrated platform to interrogate tumor and immune regulatory circuits in vivo, ex vivo, and in clinically relevant human models. Current disease areas include breast cancer and MASH-associated hepatocellular carcinoma. We are particularly interested in candidates with research experience in tumor immunology and one or more of the following areas: immunoproteasome biology or modulation, immunopeptidomics, HLA class I antigen presentation, tumor–immune interactions, immune evasion, cancer immunology, or related areas. Experience with mass spectrometry–based immunopeptidomics, antigen-processing and presentation pathways, single-cell or spatial genomics, functional genetic screening, organoid models, or in vivo tumor models would be especially valuable. The successful candidate will have the opportunity to develop mechanistic and translational projects aimed at identifying how tumors alter antigen presentation and immune recognition and at developing innovative immunotherapeutic strategies for breast cancer and MASH-associated hepatocellular carcinoma. Candidates excited about high-impact science at the intersection of cancer biology, immunology, antigen presentation, and cutting-edge genomics are strongly encouraged to apply.   Essential Duties and Responsibilities Conduct experiments including cell separation, experiments requiring molecular and cellular as well as sterile techniques, or flow cytometry, immunohistochemistry, RNA and protein undefined. Conduct experiments involving cancer mouse models. Collate and analyze experimental data in a timely manner. Make graphics. Maintain databases. Perform various in vitro tasks and assays (e.g. cell culture based, flow cytometry, sample preparation for spatial analysis and microbiome analysis) from cell culture, mouse tissue, and human (patient) tissue. Prepare materials and equipment necessary for laboratory tests. Perform general lab duties as assigned.   Preferred Qualifications PhD, MD, DVM, or a related doctoral degree with 4 or more years of experience post PhD. Relevant cancer immunology experience in and immunopeptidomics or cancer vaccines. Strong background in mouse models, mouse handling, cancer immunology, human organoid growth.  Research experience in one or more of the following areas: immunoproteasome modulation, immunopeptidomics, HLA class I antigen presentation, tumor–immune interactions, immune evasion, and cancer immunology. Experience applying these approaches to the development of immunotherapeutic strategies, particularly in breast cancer or MASH-associated hepatocellular carcinoma, is highly desirable.    Competencies Excellent communication and scientific writing skills, a strong commitment to research excellence, and the ability to work collaboratively while mentoring students and laboratory personnel. Organization Skills, Project Management Skills, Analytical skills, Critical thinking, work ethics, excellent Interpersonal skills and ability to work in the team. Compensation will be commensurate to candidate experience. Equal Employment Opportunity The University of Louisville is an Equal Employment Opportunity employer. The University strives to provide equal employment opportunity on the basis of merit and without unlawful discrimination on the basis of race, sex, age, color, national origin, ethnicity, creed, religion, disability, genetic information, sexual orientation, gender, gender identity or expression, veteran status, marital status, or pregnancy. In accordance with the Rehabilitation Act of 1973 and the Vietnam Era Veteran Readjustment Act of 1974, the University prohibits job discrimination of individuals with disabilities, Vietnam era veterans, qualified special disabled veterans, recently separated veterans, and other protected veterans. The University acknowledges its obligations to ensure affirmative steps are taken to ensure equal employment opportunities for all employees and applicants for employment. It is the policy of the University that no employee or applicant for employment be subject to unlawful discrimination in terms of recruitment, hiring, promotion, contract, contract renewal, tenure, compensation, benefits, and/or working conditions. No employee or applicant for employment is required to endorse or condemn a specific ideology, political viewpoint, or social viewpoint to be eligible for hiring, contract renewal, tenure, or promotion. Consistent with applicable law, demographic information is collected for aggregate reporting requirements. Demographic information provided through this application is not available to hiring managers/committees and is not considered in hiring or employment decisions. Assistance and Accommodations Computers are available for application submission at the Human Resources Department located at 2315 South First Street Walk, Room 02C - Louisville, Kentucky 40292. If you require assistance or accommodation with our online application process, please contact us by email at  employment@louisville.edu  or by phone 502-852-6258."
    },
    "https://uofl.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/UofLCareerSite/job/Health-Sciences-Center/Dental-Assistant-I--Graduate-Clinic-_R109114": {
      "title": "Dental Assistant I (Graduate Clinic)",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-10-09\nFull time\n\nDepartment: Location: Health Sciences Center Time Type: Full time Worker Type: Regular Job Req ID: R109114 Minimum Requirements: High school diploma or GED and 6 months of dental assisting experience, or an equivalent combination of education, training and work experience. Completion of a formal dental assisting training program and externship acceptable. CPR certification. (2 Salaried) Position Description: The University of Louisville School of Dentistry is seeking to hire a Dental Assistant I. T he Dental Assistant I position is a chairside dental assistant in our graduate specialty dental practice area located at 501 S. Preston Street, Louisville, KY.   Benefits – The University of Louisville has an exceptional benefits package; to learn more about our benefits please visit here . Essential Duties and Responsibilities Assist providers at chairside; review and maintain patient charts; prepare operatories, instruments, supplies and paperwork prior to patient arrival. Maintain clinic, assist with screening; perform specific functions and procedures related to specialty area; instruct patients in oral hygiene. Supervise patients, mix materials, take vital signs, etc.; dispense pre-medication; instruct patients in post-treatment; handle emergencies. Cross-train with other employees in area as specified by supervisor and perform duties as assigned. Forward supply requests; maintain inventory control and stock cubicles, etc. Perform laboratory duties as assigned.  Order all outside supplies with dental vendors and create an ongoing catalog for supplies. Distribute sterilized trays and instruments; receive and prepare contaminated instruments; set up/tear down operatories to infection control guidelines; clean equipment. Scan ortho models into axiUm using the Motion view for electronic records. Other duties as assigned. Target Compensation Maximum: $26.03 Target Compensation Minimum: $17.35 Compensation will be commensurate to candidate experience. Equal Employment Opportunity The University of Louisville is an Equal Employment Opportunity employer. The University strives to provide equal employment opportunity on the basis of merit and without unlawful discrimination on the basis of race, sex, age, color, national origin, ethnicity, creed, religion, disability, genetic information, sexual orientation, gender, gender identity or expression, veteran status, marital status, or pregnancy. In accordance with the Rehabilitation Act of 1973 and the Vietnam Era Veteran Readjustment Act of 1974, the University prohibits job discrimination of individuals with disabilities, Vietnam era veterans, qualified special disabled veterans, recently separated veterans, and other protected veterans. The University acknowledges its obligations to ensure affirmative steps are taken to ensure equal employment opportunities for all employees and applicants for employment. It is the policy of the University that no employee or applicant for employment be subject to unlawful discrimination in terms of recruitment, hiring, promotion, contract, contract renewal, tenure, compensation, benefits, and/or working conditions. No employee or applicant for employment is required to endorse or condemn a specific ideology, political viewpoint, or social viewpoint to be eligible for hiring, contract renewal, tenure, or promotion. Consistent with applicable law, demographic information is collected for aggregate reporting requirements. Demographic information provided through this application is not available to hiring managers/committees and is not considered in hiring or employment decisions. Assistance and Accommodations Computers are available for application submission at the Human Resources Department located at 2315 South First Street Walk, Room 02C - Louisville, Kentucky 40292. If you require assistance or accommodation with our online application process, please contact us by email at  employment@louisville.edu  or by phone 502-852-6258."
