    "https://uofl.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/UofLCareerSite/job/Health-Sciences-Center/Associate-Professor--Open-Rank-_R109489": {
      "title": "Associate Professor (Open Rank)",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-10-06\nFull time\n\nDepartment: Microbiology Location: Health Sciences Center Time Type: Full time Worker Type: Regular Job Req ID: R109489 Position Description: The Department of Microbiology and Immunology at the University of Louisville School of Medicine invites applications for a tenured or tenure-track faculty position in IMMUNOLOGY. An appointment will be made at the rank of Assistant Professor or Associate Professor commensurate with experience. The successful applicant will receive a competitive salary and benefits package, tenure-line salary support and startup funds. Minimum Requirements Candidates must have a PhD or MD in a related field, a minimum of three years of post-doctoral experience with a record of research productivity and excellence that demonstrates the potential to lead an independent immunology research program. Desirable Qualifications Expertise in adaptive immunology is preferred, overlapping with topics such as autoimmunity, bacterial or viral host defense, cancer immunobiology, immunogenetics or neuroimmunology. Applicants should also show potential for, or demonstrated excellence in, graduate and post-graduate education. Application Process Review of applications will begin immediately upon receipt of the following documents: a cover letter describing your research achievements and plans for future studies the names and email addresses of 3 references curriculum vitae Upload these documents as a single PDF file using the “Apply” button on this page; creation of a Workday account is required. Applicants may be contacted to provide additional information, such as an extended description of research interests, depending on academic rank. Review of applications will close when the position is filled. Additional information The Department of Microbiology and Immunology is a welcoming and collaborative community of 21 primary faculty members, four of whom are full-time teachers. The remaining 18 lead research programs on topics such as bacterial and viral pathogenesis, immunization and host defense, mucosal-epithelial immunology (inflammation, interactions with microbiota, tumorigenesis), neuropathogenesis (viral and sterile inflammation, resolution of inflammation), and tumor immunobiology. Entering PhD students are supported for two years by the university’s Graduate School and Independent Program in Biomedical Sciences. Thirty students are currently enrolled in the department’s graduate curriculum. The successful applicant will receive dedicated laboratory space in the Clinical and Translational Research Building, located on the University of Louisville’s downtown campus (the Health Sciences Center ). Research support is extensive, including core facilities for confocal microscopy, flow cytometry, genomics, metagenomics, live cell imaging, animal housing with germ-free facilities, whole animal imaging, and one of the country’s 11 regional biocontainment laboratories (equipped with BSL-3 and ABSL-3 facilities). The University of Louisville is a premier metropolitan university in the Commonwealth of Kentucky’s public education system; it is classified by the Carnegie Classification of Institutions of Higher Education as R1 (Very High Research Spending and Doctorate Production). Visit this link for more information about the university and its vibrant host city. Equal Employment Opportunity The University of Louisville is an Equal Employment Opportunity employer. The University strives to provide equal employment opportunity on the basis of merit and without unlawful discrimination on the basis of race, sex, age, color, national origin, ethnicity, creed, religion, disability, genetic information, sexual orientation, gender, gender identity or expression, veteran status, marital status, or pregnancy. In accordance with the Rehabilitation Act of 1973 and the Vietnam Era Veteran Readjustment Act of 1974, the University prohibits job discrimination of individuals with disabilities, Vietnam era veterans, qualified special disabled veterans, recently separated veterans, and other protected veterans. The University acknowledges its obligations to ensure affirmative steps are taken to ensure equal employment opportunities for all employees and applicants for employment. It is the policy of the University that no employee or applicant for employment be subject to unlawful discrimination in terms of recruitment, hiring, promotion, contract, contract renewal, tenure, compensation, benefits, and/or working conditions. No employee or applicant for employment is required to endorse or condemn a specific ideology, political viewpoint, or social viewpoint to be eligible for hiring, contract renewal, tenure, or promotion. Consistent with applicable law, demographic information is collected for aggregate reporting requirements. Demographic information provided through this application is not available to hiring managers/committees and is not considered in hiring or employment decisions. Assistance and Accommodations Computers are available for application submission at the Human Resources Department located at 2315 South First Street Walk, Room 02C - Louisville, Kentucky 40292. If you require assistance or accommodation with our online application process, please contact us by email at  employment@louisville.edu  or by phone 502-852-6258."
    },
    "https://uofl.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/UofLCareerSite/job/Health-Sciences-Center/Clinical-Care-Coordinator-III_R109406": {
      "title": "Clinical Care Coordinator III",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-10-06\nFull time\n\nDepartment: Location: Health Sciences Center Time Type: Full time Worker Type: Regular Job Req ID: R109406 Minimum Requirements: Associate's Degree in a related field and two (2) years of relevant experience or an equivalent combination of education and experience. Grade 3 (Hourly) Position Description: The Trager Institute is seeking a dedicated and compassionate Clinical Care Coordinator III to join our team to make a meaningful impact on the lives of older adults. Responsibilities include ensuring programs align with the latest evidence-based practices and meet the specific needs of individuals with various health issues. Job duties focus on care coordination for older adults, addressing social determinants of health, and delivering education and training to patients, caregivers, and supportive care workers.   This position is grant funded and is contingent upon the renewal of those funds.   Essential Duties and Responsibilities Coordinate case management services helping bridge the gap between clinical care and community resources. Provide support to older adults and their families helping them navigate healthcare systems and access community resources. Advocate for the needs and rights of older adults within the healthcare system and community. Supervise and mentor social work students during their field placements, providing guidance, feedback, and professional development opportunities. Participate in data collection methods and work closely with the data team to ensure outcomes are being met. Deliver comprehensive training programs on dementia care for healthcare and supportive care workers, enhancing their skills and understanding of ADRD. Monitor and evaluate the effectiveness of dementia care programs, making adjustments as needed to improve outcomes for individuals with ADRD. Ensure all programs and services comply with relevant regulations and standards, maintaining high-quality care for individuals with ADRD. Preferred Qualifications Bachelor's degree in social work or related work. Experience with data collection and reporting is preferred. Previous work with members of the community and older adults, understanding of older adult needs and healthcare needs, knowledge of various local community resources and services.   Competencies Ability to remain flexible. Strong understanding of age-friendly healthcare principles and practices. Excellent communication and interpersonal skills. Ability to work independently and as part of a multidisciplinary team. Organized and task-oriented with attention to detail in order to successfully manage a project. Great teamwork skills and be able to lead a team, direct, and mentor. Able to delegate and follow up to ensure timely completion of all duties. Excellent interpersonal skills and the ability to communicate effectively to build relationships working toward a desired outcome. Able to analyze and evaluate the success of all strategies and be flexible enough to recommend changes in direction when needed. Strong critical thinking skills and able to think through challenges, with suggested solutions. Able to build strong working relationships with all colleagues, community members and the Trager team.  Please note that any selected candidate's hire date will be effective no earlier than January 5, 2027. Compensation will be commensurate to candidate experience. Equal Employment Opportunity The University of Louisville is an Equal Employment Opportunity employer. The University strives to provide equal employment opportunity on the basis of merit and without unlawful discrimination on the basis of race, sex, age, color, national origin, ethnicity, creed, religion, disability, genetic information, sexual orientation, gender, gender identity or expression, veteran status, marital status, or pregnancy. In accordance with the Rehabilitation Act of 1973 and the Vietnam Era Veteran Readjustment Act of 1974, the University prohibits job discrimination of individuals with disabilities, Vietnam era veterans, qualified special disabled veterans, recently separated veterans, and other protected veterans. The University acknowledges its obligations to ensure affirmative steps are taken to ensure equal employment opportunities for all employees and applicants for employment. It is the policy of the University that no employee or applicant for employment be subject to unlawful discrimination in terms of recruitment, hiring, promotion, contract, contract renewal, tenure, compensation, benefits, and/or working conditions. No employee or applicant for employment is required to endorse or condemn a specific ideology, political viewpoint, or social viewpoint to be eligible for hiring, contract renewal, tenure, or promotion. Consistent with applicable law, demographic information is collected for aggregate reporting requirements. Demographic information provided through this application is not available to hiring managers/committees and is not considered in hiring or employment decisions. Assistance and Accommodations Computers are available for application submission at the Human Resources Department located at 2315 South First Street Walk, Room 02C - Louisville, Kentucky 40292. If you require assistance or accommodation with our online application process, please contact us by email at  employment@louisville.edu  or by phone 502-852-6258."
    },
    "https://uofl.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/UofLCareerSite/job/Health-Sciences-Center/Department-Coordinator-II_R109501": {
      "title": "Department Coordinator II",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-10-06\nFull time\n\nDepartment: Location: Health Sciences Center Time Type: Full time Worker Type: Regular Job Req ID: R109501 Minimum Requirements: High School Diploma/GED and a minimum of two (2) years of relevant experience or an equivalent combination of education and experience. Grade 2 (Hourly) Position Description: The primary responsibility of the Department Coordinator II is to provide high-level, comprehensive administrative support the Division of Neonatal Medicine. This individual will schedule meetings, update licensures, arrange travel requests, registrations, process financial and other administrative tasks as needed by program faculty and staff.  The Department Coordinator II is expected to be extremely organized and able to change focus of tasks/work as needed.  This position may also assist Division of Neonatology with research studies, data collection, and formatting of statistical reports for the Division Chiefs, Administration, and/or granting agencies.    Essential Duties and Responsibilities Administrative Responsibilities Identify key issues and act as a liaison with the department to resolve problems.  Oversee comprehensive administrative duties for the Division, maintain academic schedules, and provide general administrative support.    Work closely with the Division Chief and Departmental Faculty Affairs Coordinator with yearly work assignments and annual reviews.  Oversee travel arrangements for faculty in coordination with NCMG Practice Manager. Manage travel, interviews, and presentations for faculty candidates. Prepare reports and materials for unit meetings. Assist with Annual Space Survey. Coordinate NRP training sessions. Facilitate communication between medical students and attendings regarding students acting intern (AI) rotations in the NCH/ULH NICUs. Financial Responsibilities Submit purchase requests and make procurement card purchases. Process travel vouchers, and reimbursements for faculty and fellows. Human Resources Oversee the recruitment, onboarding, and hiring of all new faculty and maintain necessary employee records, including updating folios/CVs. Keep faculty informed of Dept. of Pediatrics and UofL policies and procedures about HR issues. Fellowship Program Support Collaborate and work directly with the Supervisor, Medical Residency Program to provide support to the fellowship program. ·Manage appointments and meetings with candidates and existing fellows. Coordinate and assist with the recruitment process, interviews and onboarding of fellows as necessary. Maintain program and fellow records in direct support of the Supervisor, Medical Residency Program. In partnership with fellowship leaders coordinate program activities such as conferences and meetings.   Competencies Clerical  — Knowledge of administrative and clerical procedures and systems such as word processing, managing files and records, stenography and transcription, designing forms, and other office procedures and terminology. Demonstrated proficiency in Microsoft Outlook, Excel, Word, PeopleSoft, and Microsoft Teams/Zoom. Customer and Personal Service  — Knowledge of principles and processes for providing customer and personal services. This includes customer needs assessment, meeting quality standards for services, and evaluation of customer satisfaction. Demonstrates a strong commitment to customer service excellence, including assessing customer needs, meeting quality standards, and ensuring customer satisfaction. Administration and Management  — Knowledge of business and management principles involved in strategic planning, resource allocation, human resources modeling, leadership technique, production methods, and coordination of people and resources. Communications and Media  — Knowledge of media production, communication, and dissemination techniques and methods. This includes alternative ways to inform and entertain via written, oral, interpersonal communication and visual media effectively. Service Excellence - Responsive, informs constituents of process, pleasant to work with, educates, and provides timely, accurate information. Organized - Manages time effectively, keeps tasks appropriately prioritized Work Ethic - Motivated, diligent and persistent in the workplace, stay on tasks to completion and work fast to ensure optimal efficiency. Please note that any selected candidate's hire date may be effective no earlier than January 5, 2027. Target Compensation Maximum: $26.03 Target Compensation Minimum: $17.35 Compensation will be commensurate to candidate experience. Equal Employment Opportunity The University of Louisville is an Equal Employment Opportunity employer. The University strives to provide equal employment opportunity on the basis of merit and without unlawful discrimination on the basis of race, sex, age, color, national origin, ethnicity, creed, religion, disability, genetic information, sexual orientation, gender, gender identity or expression, veteran status, marital status, or pregnancy. In accordance with the Rehabilitation Act of 1973 and the Vietnam Era Veteran Readjustment Act of 1974, the University prohibits job discrimination of individuals with disabilities, Vietnam era veterans, qualified special disabled veterans, recently separated veterans, and other protected veterans. The University acknowledges its obligations to ensure affirmative steps are taken to ensure equal employment opportunities for all employees and applicants for employment. It is the policy of the University that no employee or applicant for employment be subject to unlawful discrimination in terms of recruitment, hiring, promotion, contract, contract renewal, tenure, compensation, benefits, and/or working conditions. No employee or applicant for employment is required to endorse or condemn a specific ideology, political viewpoint, or social viewpoint to be eligible for hiring, contract renewal, tenure, or promotion. Consistent with applicable law, demographic information is collected for aggregate reporting requirements. Demographic information provided through this application is not available to hiring managers/committees and is not considered in hiring or employment decisions. Assistance and Accommodations Computers are available for application submission at the Human Resources Department located at 2315 South First Street Walk, Room 02C - Louisville, Kentucky 40292. If you require assistance or accommodation with our online application process, please contact us by email at  employment@louisville.edu  or by phone 502-852-6258."
    },
    "https://uofl.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/UofLCareerSite/job/Health-Sciences-Center/Department-Coordinator-IV_R109484-2": {
      "title": "Department Coordinator IV",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-10-06\nFull time\n\nDepartment: Location: Health Sciences Center Time Type: Full time Worker Type: Regular Job Req ID: R109484 Minimum Requirements: Bachelor's degree in a related field or an equivalent combination of education and experience. Grade 4 (Hourly) Position Description: The Department Coordinator IV provides executive, administrative, and operational support for the Department of Pathology & Laboratory Medicine. This position serves as a key executive support resource to the Department Chair and departmental leadership, coordinating high-level priorities, confidential communications, leadership meetings, strategic initiatives, and cross-functional departmental activities. The role requires substantial independent judgment, discretion, professionalism, and the ability to anticipate needs, manage competing priorities, and coordinate complex administrative and operational workflows. The Department Coordinator IV serves as a primary liaison between departmental leadership, faculty, trainees, staff, institutional partners, clinical partners, and external contacts to facilitate timely communication, effective decision support, and successful execution of departmental priorities. This position supports executive-level planning, preparation, coordination, follow-up, and documentation for departmental leadership activities, including strategic initiatives, academic operations, clinical service support, institutional reporting, special projects, and sensitive personnel-related matters. The position is expected to operate with a high degree of initiative, confidentiality, and accountability in support of departmental goals. Essential Duties and Responsibilities Executive Leadership Support Provide high-level executive administrative support to the Department Chair and designated departmental leaders. Manage the Department Chair's complex calendar, scheduling priorities, executive commitments, and competing internal and external requests. Serve as a gatekeeper and liaison for the Chair's office, independently assessing, prioritizing, and routing requests, communications, and time-sensitive matters. Coordinate meetings involving departmental leadership, faculty, institutional executives, clinical partners, external stakeholders, and visiting guests. Prepare agendas, briefing materials, background documents, summaries, talking points, correspondence, and follow-up items for leadership meetings and decision-making. Track leadership priorities, commitments, deadlines, and action items to ensure timely follow-through and appropriate escalation. Maintain strict confidentiality in support of sensitive faculty, staff, trainee, operational, financial, compliance, and personnel-related matters. Exercise independent judgment in identifying issues requiring leadership attention and coordinating appropriate next steps. Executive Communications and Correspondence Draft, edit, format, and coordinate executive-level correspondence, reports, memoranda, presentations, institutional communications, and official departmental documents. Prepare concise summaries, background materials, data requests, and briefing documents to support leadership decisions and external communications. Coordinate communications on behalf of the Chair and departmental leadership with professionalism, accuracy, discretion, and appropriate tone. Support preparation of materials for institutional leadership meetings, faculty communications, departmental updates, accreditation-related activities, and strategic planning efforts. Ensure written materials and communications reflect departmental priorities, institutional standards, and appropriate confidentiality. Strategic Planning and Special Project Coordination Provide administrative and project coordination support for departmental strategic planning, retreats, leadership initiatives, operational improvement efforts, and special projects. Coordinate project timelines, deliverables, meeting materials, stakeholder input, and follow-up documentation for departmental initiatives. Monitor progress on assigned leadership priorities and provide regular updates to the Chair and departmental leaders. Assist in gathering, organizing, and analyzing information needed for executive decision-making, reporting, planning, and implementation. Support development and maintenance of departmental dashboards, tracking tools, reports, and project documentation as assigned. Coordinate initiatives that involve multiple operational areas, including academic programs, clinical services, education, research, and administration. Faculty and Academic Affairs Support Coordinate administrative activities supporting faculty academic, clinical, research, educational, and professional responsibilities. Draft, edit, and coordinate faculty correspondence, letters, reports, presentations, manuscripts, scholarly materials, and academic submissions. Maintain and update faculty curriculum vitae, biosketches, academic records, scholarly activity documentation, and institutional reporting system entries, including Elements. Track departmental publications, academic productivity, faculty accomplishments, and professional activities for reporting and recognition. Provide administrative coordination for education programs, including medical student, residency, fellowship, and faculty development activities. Clinical and Operational Leadership Support Support departmental clinical operations through coordination of schedules, coverage planning, workflow documentation, policy updates, and communication with clinical partners. Facilitate communication between faculty, laboratory leadership, administrative staff, and institutional partners to support clinical service priorities. Support development, implementation, and maintenance of departmental policies, procedures, operational workflows, and standard administrative processes. Assist with tracking operational issues, follow-up items, and process improvement opportunities requiring departmental leadership attention. Coordinate documentation and communication related to compliance, institutional requirements, and department-level operational initiatives. Meeting, Committee, and Event Coordination Coordinate high-level departmental, committee, faculty, leadership, and program meetings, including scheduling, agenda development, materials preparation, logistics, documentation, and follow-up. Prepare, record, and distribute meeting minutes, summaries, action items, and decision logs. Maintain tracking systems for meeting outcomes, assigned action items, deadlines, and leadership follow-up. Coordinate departmental retreats, faculty meetings, recruitment visits, conferences, educational events, guest visits, and special programs. Manage event logistics, communications, speaker coordination, facility needs, audiovisual arrangements, catering, travel coordination, and post-event follow-up as needed. Department Operations and Administrative Management Serve as an administrative resource for departmental operations, executive workflow management, and cross-functional coordination. Coordinate internal departmental communications, shared calendars, administrative resources, and leadership tracking tools. Identify opportunities to improve administrative processes, communication flow, documentation, and operational efficiency. Support administrative alignment across departmental missions, including clinical care, education, research, faculty affairs, and service. Provide guidance and coordination to support staff or student workers as assigned. Perform other executive, administrative, and operational duties as assigned in support of departmental priorities. General Administrative Responsibilities Provide professional, responsive, and solution-oriented service to faculty, staff, trainees, institutional partners, external stakeholders, and visitors. Maintain departmental records, files, shared documents, administrative systems, and confidential materials in accordance with university and health system guidelines. Ensure appropriate handling of sensitive information and compliance with university, health system, HIPAA, and confidentiality standards. Use Microsoft Office Suite, Teams, Outlook, institutional systems, and other administrative tools to support efficient communication, documentation, tracking, and reporting. Demonstrate professionalism, discretion, initiative, sound judgment, adaptability, and accountability in all aspects of the role. Preferred Qualifications Bachelor's degree in business administration, healthcare administration, communications, public administration, higher education administration, or a related field. Experience providing executive-level support to a senior leader, chair, dean, director, executive administrator, or equivalent leadership role. Experience working in an academic medical center, healthcare system, university, or complex administrative environment. Experience managing complex calendars, confidential communications, executive correspondence, meeting preparation, and leadership follow-up. Experience coordinating projects, leadership initiatives, faculty affairs, academic programs, clinical operations, or institutional reporting. ·   Competencies Strong writing, editing, organizational, prioritization, and project coordination skills. Ability to exercise independent judgment, maintain confidentiality, anticipate leadership needs, and manage multiple high-priority tasks with limited supervision. Proficiency with Microsoft Office Suite, Teams, Outlook, PowerPoint, Excel, Word, Adobe, and institutional administrative systems. Please note that any selected candidate's hire date will be effective no earlier than January 5, 2027 Compensation will be commensurate to candidate experience. Equal Employment Opportunity The University of Louisville is an Equal Employment Opportunity employer. The University strives to provide equal employment opportunity on the basis of merit and without unlawful discrimination on the basis of race, sex, age, color, national origin, ethnicity, creed, religion, disability, genetic information, sexual orientation, gender, gender identity or expression, veteran status, marital status, or pregnancy. In accordance with the Rehabilitation Act of 1973 and the Vietnam Era Veteran Readjustment Act of 1974, the University prohibits job discrimination of individuals with disabilities, Vietnam era veterans, qualified special disabled veterans, recently separated veterans, and other protected veterans. The University acknowledges its obligations to ensure affirmative steps are taken to ensure equal employment opportunities for all employees and applicants for employment. It is the policy of the University that no employee or applicant for employment be subject to unlawful discrimination in terms of recruitment, hiring, promotion, contract, contract renewal, tenure, compensation, benefits, and/or working conditions. No employee or applicant for employment is required to endorse or condemn a specific ideology, political viewpoint, or social viewpoint to be eligible for hiring, contract renewal, tenure, or promotion. Consistent with applicable law, demographic information is collected for aggregate reporting requirements. Demographic information provided through this application is not available to hiring managers/committees and is not considered in hiring or employment decisions. Assistance and Accommodations Computers are available for application submission at the Human Resources Department located at 2315 South First Street Walk, Room 02C - Louisville, Kentucky 40292. If you require assistance or accommodation with our online application process, please contact us by email at  employment@louisville.edu  or by phone 502-852-6258."
    },
    "https://uofl.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/UofLCareerSite/job/Belknap-Campus/IT-Systems-Engineer-V--Cybersecurity-Engineer-_R109233": {
      "title": "IT Systems Engineer V (Cybersecurity Engineer)",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-10-06\nFull time\n\nDepartment: Location: Belknap Campus Time Type: Full time Worker Type: Regular Job Req ID: R109233 Minimum Requirements: Bachelor's degree in Computer Science or a related field and eight (8) years of relevant experience or an equivalent combination of education and experience. Grade10 (Salaried) Position Description: The University of Louisville is seeking an experienced and service-oriented IT Systems Engineer V (Cybersecurity Engineer) to design, implement, and maintain enterprise cybersecurity systems and infrastructure. This position will serve as a senior technical resource for security engineering, helping protect university information assets, networks, cloud environments, and research systems against evolving threats while supporting regulatory and higher education security requirements. The successful candidate will demonstrate advanced proficiency in enterprise security engineering, complemented by a collaborative approach and the ability to translate complex technical risks and controls into practical, supportable solutions. Experience in higher education, research computing, or clinical and operational security environments is a plus. This job may require access to technology, data and software source code that is subject to US export controls. An offer of employment is contingent upon verifying U.S.-person status or obtaining the necessary export control licenses to perform the job functions. Key responsibilities for this role include: This is a hybrid role, combining remote work with on-site presence as needed. Plan, implement, and maintain technical security measures, controls, platforms, and engineering solutions across university infrastructure. Configure, operate, and maintain enterprise security technologies, including firewalls, IDS/IPS, SIEM, EDR, DLP, and vulnerability management platforms. Monitor, identify, and mitigate security risks, and lead technical response activities during cybersecurity incidents and investigations. Engineer and manage identity and access management services, including directory services, single sign-on, multi-factor authentication, and privileged access management. Develop and maintain security automation and orchestration capabilities to improve detection, response, and operational efficiency. Design and support enterprise security architectures, including network segmentation, zero trust frameworks, cloud security posture management, certificate lifecycle management, and PKI. Conduct security architecture reviews for new systems, applications, and cloud environments, and recommend technologies aligned with university objectives and higher education best practices. Support regulatory and contractual compliance initiatives, including HIPAA/ePHI, FERPA, PCI-DSS, CMMC, and NIST 800-171, through implementation of associated technical controls. Collaborate with security operations, network engineering, infrastructure, application, data, and research computing teams to deploy secure and resilient services. Develop and maintain architecture diagrams, runbooks, standard operating procedures, and other security engineering documentation. Mentor colleagues and serve as a technical resource for IT security projects and university technology initiatives. Remain informed about the evolving threat landscape, emerging attack vectors, security technologies, and best practices relevant to higher education. Preferred qualifications: Master’s degree in a related field. Experience with firewalls, IDS/IPS, EDR, vulnerability scanning, and SIEM technologies. Knowledge of technical infrastructure, networks, databases, and systems in relation to IT security and IT risk. Familiarity with security and compliance frameworks such as NIST, ISO, and PCI-DSS. Excellent oral, written, and interpersonal communication skills, including the ability to explain complex technical topics to a non-technical, business-focused audience. Strong leadership, collaboration, and team management skills. CISSP or another information security-related certification. Network engineering and system administration experience. Cloud security experience in Azure, AWS, or GCP, including cloud-native security services. Microsoft 365 security experience, including Microsoft Defender, Sentinel, Intune, and Purview. Experience with identity platforms such as Microsoft Entra ID and Active Directory, Duo, or Fischer Identity. Familiarity with higher education IT environments, research computing, secure enclaves, or clinical and operational security requirements. Compensation will be commensurate to candidate experience. Equal Employment Opportunity The University of Louisville is an Equal Employment Opportunity employer. The University strives to provide equal employment opportunity on the basis of merit and without unlawful discrimination on the basis of race, sex, age, color, national origin, ethnicity, creed, religion, disability, genetic information, sexual orientation, gender, gender identity or expression, veteran status, marital status, or pregnancy. In accordance with the Rehabilitation Act of 1973 and the Vietnam Era Veteran Readjustment Act of 1974, the University prohibits job discrimination of individuals with disabilities, Vietnam era veterans, qualified special disabled veterans, recently separated veterans, and other protected veterans. The University acknowledges its obligations to ensure affirmative steps are taken to ensure equal employment opportunities for all employees and applicants for employment. It is the policy of the University that no employee or applicant for employment be subject to unlawful discrimination in terms of recruitment, hiring, promotion, contract, contract renewal, tenure, compensation, benefits, and/or working conditions. No employee or applicant for employment is required to endorse or condemn a specific ideology, political viewpoint, or social viewpoint to be eligible for hiring, contract renewal, tenure, or promotion. Consistent with applicable law, demographic information is collected for aggregate reporting requirements. Demographic information provided through this application is not available to hiring managers/committees and is not considered in hiring or employment decisions. Assistance and Accommodations Computers are available for application submission at the Human Resources Department located at 2315 South First Street Walk, Room 02C - Louisville, Kentucky 40292. If you require assistance or accommodation with our online application process, please contact us by email at  employment@louisville.edu  or by phone 502-852-6258."
    },
    "https://uofl.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/UofLCareerSite/job/Belknap-Campus/Program-Manager-I_R109451": {
      "title": "Program Manager I",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-10-06\nFull time\n\nDepartment: PhD Operations and Research Location: Belknap Campus Time Type: Full time Worker Type: Regular Job Req ID: R109451 Minimum Requirements: Bachelor's degree in a related field and four (4) years of relevant experience or an equivalent combination of education and experience. Grade 7 (Salaried) Position Description: The Program Manager I at the University of Louisville Kent School of Social Work directly reports to and carries out their roles and responsibilities under the supervision and direction of the PhD program director. The Program Manager is tasked with ensuring program activities including admissions, milestone tracking, registration/advising, orientation, graduation, student success and course management. and other related ongoing activities and initiatives are accomplished in a timely manner to meet program goals and objectives. This position will engage students directly through conducting individual meetings, workshops, and professional development training to foster student retention and success. The ideal candidate will demonstrate a commitment to an equitable and inclusive environment for students, staff and faculty that promotes a culture of belonging. Essential Duties and Responsibilities Recruitment/Marketing including: Work with Kent School Marketing to develop marketing materials Periodically review marketing materials including all online and hardcopy application materials, bulletin/catalog and scholarship materials as well as materials published by The Graduate School with attention to accuracy and consistency. Assist with closing recruitment initiatives including offering face to face and online (i.e. webinar) informational sessions Assist with identifying new markets for the doctoral program, successfully collaborating with marketing offices and Kent Recruitment and Admissions personnel. Complete periodic queries and create and maintain reports on admissions and graduation data to share with administrative leadership and guide policy and priorities, including as-needed reports upon request for accrediting agencies like CSWE. Admissions: Check and respond to all questions and requests via the PHD Service Account. Advise prospective students/applicants on Kent School’s procedures and policies related to PHD admissions by offering/facilitating face to face, and online, phone and/or e-mail informational meetings. Process doctoral applications in a timely manner, adjusting work hours when necessary to accommodate peak deadline periods. Review submitted application materials for completeness and provide completed applicant admissions materials to the PhD Program Committee Utilize the university’s systems to track student admissions/records as it pertains to doctoral students. Collaborate with Kent Recruitment and Admissions staff and provide on-going communication for application updates, offers, and support to prospective students and applicants - as well as coordinate interviews with the admissions committee and selected applicants. Coordinate and communicate with other relevant units on campus such as the Graduate School, the Kent Director of Marketing and Coordinator of Continuing education, and the International Center to facilitate administrative/regulatory approval required from these units in order for prospective students to be admitted to the PhD program. Registration Updates and disseminates virtual welcome packet to incoming students Provides information on course schedules, registration, financial aid, and scholarships to students; assist students to troubleshoot registration problems/issues. Initiate and request approval for changes in courses, adding courses, i.e. all independent studies via the Course Inventory File (CIF) form and CLSS (CourseLeaf); Coordinate with registrar as needed for circumstances, batch enrollment, or removal of courses Manage the schedule and registration/enrollment of courses to ensure students are able to take the courses they need in the right sequence in collaboration with the program director to achieve strategic course management goals; update catalog and schedule as required using university systems Provides information on course schedules, registration, financial aid, and scholarships to students Orientation and other events: Coordinate the departmental student orientation for the PhD program in an online and in-person capacity, partnering with campus resources, and contribute to the development of curriculum/agenda for orientation and any accompanying student receptions, open houses, and student information sessions. Coordinate PhD Bootcamp specifically in addition to orientation Coordinate Dissertations, Comp Exam, and PhD commencement celebration activities in coordination with the DSW Program Manager. Retention Conducts Monthly to biweekly virtual office hours/optional meetings for students to attend to address concerns and promote planning (students can optionally submit a concern topic to review in the meeting). Additionally conduct end of semester meetings. Oversee the process from prospective students to applicants through matriculation and graduation Communicate with the Director about those circumstances in need of action or coordinate professional and academic review. Document revised planning or safety plan if needed. Notify appropriate parties if needed (i.e. DOS, mentor, etc) Collaborate with the doctoral Program Director and Associate Dean of Academic Affairs to develop, promote, and support initiatives to strengthen student retention. Serve as the departmental point person on doctoral program student retention and satisfaction to ensure achievement of PhD retention goals. Coordinate closely with the PhD Program Director, Associate Dean of Academic Affairs, and other facilitating parties in all matters relating to enrollment and retention. Ongoing Maintain all required documents/credentials for PhD students. Assist with comprehensive exam and dissertation processes and procedures coordinated with the Graduate School; Track student progress to degree milestones Assist with, coordinate, and communicate program Annual Mentoring Contracts and Annual Review procedures to students and mentors Coordinate Professional Development Workshops and other service committee opportunities for students and candidates to participate. Communicate with PhD students and the representative on programmatic changes, news, etc. via the program’s weekly update newsletter Assist with development and update/upkeep of the program manuals to be used as a resource for prospective and current students. Collaboratively manage and oversee the preparation of the program portion of the college catalog and related publications for the PhD program where needed (Kent School webpage, Blackboard, etc.) for appropriate messaging and brand identity in collaboration with the Director of Marketing. Coordinate and communicate with other relevant units on campus such as the Graduate School, Registrar's Office, and International Center on matters related to the PhD program. Coordinate and communicate with other relevant units in relation to various events throughout the semester (i.e. Graduate Appreciation Week; Social Work Week) Assist with scholarship applications and maintain an accurate corresponding database. Provide information to faculty and other committee members on prospective scholarship recipients. Attend and coordinate PhD faculty meetings, follow up with Program Director on unfinished business items or put them on future agendas, prepare written minutes and disseminate to faculty in a timely basis. Prepare timely reports on recruitment, scholarship, admission, financial aid, registration, graduation and alumni accomplishments for the program, school, university, and relevant national organizations (i.e., CSWE, GADE). Track and report all assistantships to the Graduate School adhering to all guidelines pertaining to Graduate assistantships, including foreign nationals, housing, transportation, parking permits, travel information, etc. Communicate with GRA/Fellowship Scholarship students to submit appropriate waivers and department funding requests Track the timely confirmation of faculty mentors for student independent studies, research and teaching practicums communicating with the Director those circumstances in need of action. Track and assist with Program Operating Budget relating to Office Supplies, Travel (conferences), Instruction, and other related components. Track and assist with Endowment/Gift accounts to ensure funding is used appropriately to assist with student scholarship, professional development, dissertation/research and other program related support Track student progress in the program and adherence to the program plan, and communicating with the Director those circumstances in need of action Periodically research/scan other benchmark and well-established programs to ensure program parity, state of the art educational programming and training, and current practice trends. Analyzes and synthesizes quantitative and qualitative data to implement innovative practices and strategies around retention and student success. Regularly presents and communicates these data to PhD Program Director and other stakeholders for outcome-based initiatives and accreditation. Assist with ongoing evaluation of the PhD program by gathering student and instructor feedback data Communicate weekly with PhD students and their representatives on programmatic changes, news Represent the school on various university committees and external committees. Coordinate the Psychosocial Oncology concentration by maintaining a student database of current and past students, communicating via email and scheduling meetings with students. Plan the fall meeting of oncology researchers by reserving space, accommodations, itineraries, agendas, restaurant reservations, and other materials. Print surveys and assemble questionnaires. Assist faculty in the specialization with any teaching-related needs, i.e. room scheduling, textbooks, etc. Assist Endowed Chair with marketing related planning and recruitment. Assist with course or new program development processes as needed in correspondence with Office of Academic Planning and Accountability (OAPA); This includes but is not limited to course or degree proposals, changes in codes or course descriptions, deletion/activation of courses Assist with development of marketing materials and correspondence with Marketing Department; Assist with PSO Website improvements that relate to PSO Chair, course/course description, and other related updates Preferred Qualifications Earned Masters in higher education administration Evidence of successful coordination of academic programs Evidence of successfully working with students, faculty, and colleagues in an academic or related setting Evidence of working independently and taking initiative to accomplish program objectives Demonstrated competence in the use of computer, software (i.e. Word, Excel, Access, PowerPoint, Publisher) and other technological tools to facilitate efficient and creative program coordination At least two (2) years of experience in a higher education setting at the post-baccalaureate and/or graduate level. Demonstrating increasing levels of responsibility, professional expertise, and leadership in higher education Knowledge of EAB Analytics, Sonia, and Workday. Competencies Proactive - anticipates and plans for problems before they arise. Flexible – ability to change directions as needed for the good of the department or program. Service Excellence – responsive, informs constituents of process, pleasant to work with, educates and provides timely, accurate information. Organized – manages time effectively, keeps tasks appropriately prioritized. Critical Thinking – ability to think through issues and identify appropriate options. Work Ethic – motivated, diligent, industrious and persistent in the workplace, stays on tasks to completion, works at a fast pace to ensure optimal efficiency. Interpersonal – can build effective, strong working relationships with employees, colleagues, management and vendors through trust, communication, and credibility. Teamwork – ability to work with others, serve others, help others, mentor take directions from others in the interest of moving process and programs forward to the desired outcome to accomplish the goals and objectives for each program. Skills: The individual must have strong verbal and written skills to communicate with prospective students, applicants, students, faculty, staff, and community partners as well as conduct face to face and virtual presentations and conduct meetings. Target Compensation Maximum: $91,721.00 Target Compensation Minimum: $61,147.00 Compensation will be commensurate to candidate experience. Equal Employment Opportunity The University of Louisville is an Equal Employment Opportunity employer. The University strives to provide equal employment opportunity on the basis of merit and without unlawful discrimination on the basis of race, sex, age, color, national origin, ethnicity, creed, religion, disability, genetic information, sexual orientation, gender, gender identity or expression, veteran status, marital status, or pregnancy. In accordance with the Rehabilitation Act of 1973 and the Vietnam Era Veteran Readjustment Act of 1974, the University prohibits job discrimination of individuals with disabilities, Vietnam era veterans, qualified special disabled veterans, recently separated veterans, and other protected veterans. The University acknowledges its obligations to ensure affirmative steps are taken to ensure equal employment opportunities for all employees and applicants for employment. It is the policy of the University that no employee or applicant for employment be subject to unlawful discrimination in terms of recruitment, hiring, promotion, contract, contract renewal, tenure, compensation, benefits, and/or working conditions. No employee or applicant for employment is required to endorse or condemn a specific ideology, political viewpoint, or social viewpoint to be eligible for hiring, contract renewal, tenure, or promotion. Consistent with applicable law, demographic information is collected for aggregate reporting requirements. Demographic information provided through this application is not available to hiring managers/committees and is not considered in hiring or employment decisions. Assistance and Accommodations Computers are available for application submission at the Human Resources Department located at 2315 South First Street Walk, Room 02C - Louisville, Kentucky 40292. If you require assistance or accommodation with our online application process, please contact us by email at  employment@louisville.edu  or by phone 502-852-6258."
    },
    "https://uofl.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/UofLCareerSite/job/Health-Sciences-Center/Research-Scientist_R109493": {
      "title": "Research Scientist",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-10-06\nFull time\n\nDepartment: Location: Health Sciences Center Time Type: Full time Worker Type: Regular Job Req ID: R109493 Minimum Requirements: Master's degree in a related field and four (4) years of relevant experience or an equivalent combination of education and experience. Grade 7 (Salaried) Position Description: The Pediatric Research Institute, Department of Pediatrics, University of Louisville School of Medicine, is seeking a highly motivated Research Scientist to contribute to research projects supported by the Kentucky Pediatric Cancer Research Trust Fund. These projects focus on investigating the genetic and transcriptomic mechanisms underlying neuroinflammatory diseases, including pediatric central nervous system (CNS) tumors. The successful candidate will be responsible for conducting a variety of molecular biology and translational research activities, including RNA, DNA, and protein isolation from human and murine tissues; quality control assessment; sequencing library preparation; in situ hybridization (ISH)-based assays, including fluorescence in situ hybridization (FISH) and related spatial transcriptomic techniques; qPCR primer design and validation; data analysis and interpretation; and preparation of progress reports and scientific presentations. The individual will be expected to maintain the highest standards of research integrity, rigor, and reproducibility. In partnership with the PI, the successful candidate will assist in overseeing daily laboratory operations and bench-side research activities. Responsibilities will include maintaining accurate research records, managing laboratory inventories and supply procurement, organizing and consolidating experimental data, and preparing research reports. Strong laboratory management and organizational skills are essential. The position requires strict adherence to institutional and laboratory safety policies and procedures, including those governing the handling of hazardous materials, biological specimens, and specialized research equipment.    Essential Duties and Responsibilities Conduct molecular biology and translational research experiments supporting studies of neuroinflammatory diseases and pediatric CNS tumors. Perform RNA, DNA, and protein isolation; quality control assessments; sequencing library preparation; and ISH/FISH-based assays. Design and validate qPCR assays and assist with experimental protocol optimization. Analyze, organize, and maintain experimental data and prepare research reports and presentations. Assist the Principal Investigator with grant preparation, renewal, and submission activities. Train and mentor students, fellows, technicians, and visiting scholars in laboratory methods and research best practices. Assist in coordinating daily laboratory operations, including recordkeeping, supply management, and equipment maintenance. Ensure compliance with laboratory safety requirements and uphold standards of research integrity and rigor. Perform other related duties as assigned.     Preferred Qualifications Ph.D. in Neuroscience, Molecular Biology, Genetics, Cancer Biology, Biomedical Sciences, or a related field. Experience with next-generation sequencing (NGS) workflows, library preparation, and transcriptomic data generation. Proficiency in molecular biology techniques, including nucleic acid and protein isolation, qPCR, and ISH/FISH-based assays. Experience working with human and/or animal tissue samples in a research laboratory setting. Experience mentoring students, trainees, or laboratory personnel. Strong organizational, communication, and laboratory management skills. Experience with grant-related activities, including preparation of preliminary data and scientific reports, is desirable.   Competencies Strong scientific reasoning, analytical, and problem-solving skills. Ability to design, execute, and troubleshoot research experiments. Strong attention to detail and commitment to data quality, accuracy, and reproducibility. Excellent organizational and time-management skills with the ability to manage multiple projects and deadlines. Effective written and verbal communication skills, including scientific presentation and reporting. Ability to work collaboratively in a multidisciplinary research environment. Demonstrated leadership, mentoring, and team-building abilities. Commitment to research integrity, professionalism, and ethical scientific conduct. Knowledge of laboratory safety practices and regulatory compliance requirements. Please note that any selected candidate's hire date may be effective no earlier than January 5, 2027 Target Compensation Maximum: $91,721.00 Target Compensation Minimum: $61,147.00 Compensation will be commensurate to candidate experience. Equal Employment Opportunity The University of Louisville is an Equal Employment Opportunity employer. The University strives to provide equal employment opportunity on the basis of merit and without unlawful discrimination on the basis of race, sex, age, color, national origin, ethnicity, creed, religion, disability, genetic information, sexual orientation, gender, gender identity or expression, veteran status, marital status, or pregnancy. In accordance with the Rehabilitation Act of 1973 and the Vietnam Era Veteran Readjustment Act of 1974, the University prohibits job discrimination of individuals with disabilities, Vietnam era veterans, qualified special disabled veterans, recently separated veterans, and other protected veterans. The University acknowledges its obligations to ensure affirmative steps are taken to ensure equal employment opportunities for all employees and applicants for employment. It is the policy of the University that no employee or applicant for employment be subject to unlawful discrimination in terms of recruitment, hiring, promotion, contract, contract renewal, tenure, compensation, benefits, and/or working conditions. No employee or applicant for employment is required to endorse or condemn a specific ideology, political viewpoint, or social viewpoint to be eligible for hiring, contract renewal, tenure, or promotion. Consistent with applicable law, demographic information is collected for aggregate reporting requirements. Demographic information provided through this application is not available to hiring managers/committees and is not considered in hiring or employment decisions. Assistance and Accommodations Computers are available for application submission at the Human Resources Department located at 2315 South First Street Walk, Room 02C - Louisville, Kentucky 40292. If you require assistance or accommodation with our online application process, please contact us by email at  employment@louisville.edu  or by phone 502-852-6258."
    },
    "https://uofl.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/UofLCareerSite/job/Belknap-Campus/Student-Success-Specialist-III--KCTCS-Pathways-Specialist-_R109459": {
      "title": "Student Success Specialist III (KCTCS Pathways Specialist)",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-10-06\nFull time\n\nDepartment: Location: Belknap Campus Time Type: Full time Worker Type: Regular Job Req ID: R109459 Minimum Requirements: Bachelor's degree in a related field and four (4) years of relevant experience or an equivalent combination of education and experience. Grade 5 (Salaried) Position Description: The University of Louisville’s ULtra team is seeking a motivated individual as the Student Success Specialist (KCTCS Pathways Specialist) who will oversee management, tracking, and facilitation of the KCTCS Pathways project. Ideal candidate will also serve as the Transfer lead for promoting and student engagement of the KCTCS Pathways project.  This position will be responsible for developing and facilitating a routine process to maintain updated KCTCS Pathways. Ideal candidate will collaborate with members of University of Louisville and Kentucky Community & Technical College’s curriculum management team(s) to ensure that all pathways are updated on a routine basis. The ideal candidate for this position will also be responsible for expanding student engagement through promotion of the Pathways project. This role combines knowledge of KCTCS to four-year transfer curriculum management, organizational, customer service, and communication skills. Position will be fully remote with occasional on-site meetings- ideal candidate must reside in commutable distance to Belknap campus in Louisville Kentucky   Essential Duties and Responsibilities   Create and maintain routine updates of KCTCS Pathways documents to include creating a process to track and implement yearly updates to KCTCS Pathways. Responsible for facilitating updates to individual existing pathway documents along with creation of new pathways for all University of Louisville majors. Establish clear tracking database that outlines academic and programmatic updates on pathways that occur at KCTCS and University of Louisville. Responsible for creating a database that tracks all programmatic updates as they relate to KCTCS/UofL curricular changes based on catalog updates. Coordinate routine communication with curriculum management stakeholders at UofL and KCTCS to assist with establishment of process that routinely tracks curricular changes of pathway documents. This includes but is not limited to facilitating collaboration between KCTCS Curricular Management team and necessary academic units at UofL. Work as primary point of contact responsible for updating and posting of current and up-to-date Pathway documents to University of Louisville’s Transfer website. Serve as point of contact responsible for ensuring that all updated Pathway documents are also provided to necessary KCTCS web management point person(s). Assist with development and implementation of strategies to promote and engage KCTCS stakeholders and students to accommodate Pathways project growth. Meet with and inform prospective KCTCS transfers on KCTCS Pathways opportunities.   Preferred Qualifications Master’s Degree Familiarity with Kentucky Community & Technical College curriculum as it relates to transfer standards. Prior experience working in higher education setting specifically with transfer student populations. The ability to work independently with little supervision to implement existing and new processes to meet curricular deadlines. Demonstrate proficiency with Content Management Systems in a higher education setting, Microsoft Applications (Word, Excel, PowerPoint, Outlook, ect), online video conferencing tools such as Zoom/ Microsoft Teams. Prior knowledge of data input, management, and visualization for database use. Effective interpersonal, oral, and written communication skills. Requires attention to detail, organization, computer and software skills, and communication skills as the candidate will be responsible for managing and tracking of yearly updates to each pathway as updates are made available Candidate must be student centered, exhibit exemplary time management skills, the ability to work on a team, and must demonstrate basic knowledge of academic programming and functions. Candidate expected to serve on Kentucky’s Council on Postsecondary Education peer team to receive and provide updates as it relates to KCTCS Pathway curriculum updates.     Competencies Ability to build rapport and sustain professional relationships with colleagues and teammates.  Experience and comfort working in project management. High level of interpersonal communication, problem solving, time management, and organizational skills Familiarity with technology related to word processing and student information systems. Ability to work independently and in a collaborative setting.   Target Compensation Maximum: $72,389.00 Target Compensation Minimum: $48,225.00 Compensation will be commensurate to candidate experience. Equal Employment Opportunity The University of Louisville is an Equal Employment Opportunity employer. The University strives to provide equal employment opportunity on the basis of merit and without unlawful discrimination on the basis of race, sex, age, color, national origin, ethnicity, creed, religion, disability, genetic information, sexual orientation, gender, gender identity or expression, veteran status, marital status, or pregnancy. In accordance with the Rehabilitation Act of 1973 and the Vietnam Era Veteran Readjustment Act of 1974, the University prohibits job discrimination of individuals with disabilities, Vietnam era veterans, qualified special disabled veterans, recently separated veterans, and other protected veterans. The University acknowledges its obligations to ensure affirmative steps are taken to ensure equal employment opportunities for all employees and applicants for employment. It is the policy of the University that no employee or applicant for employment be subject to unlawful discrimination in terms of recruitment, hiring, promotion, contract, contract renewal, tenure, compensation, benefits, and/or working conditions. No employee or applicant for employment is required to endorse or condemn a specific ideology, political viewpoint, or social viewpoint to be eligible for hiring, contract renewal, tenure, or promotion. Consistent with applicable law, demographic information is collected for aggregate reporting requirements. Demographic information provided through this application is not available to hiring managers/committees and is not considered in hiring or employment decisions. Assistance and Accommodations Computers are available for application submission at the Human Resources Department located at 2315 South First Street Walk, Room 02C - Louisville, Kentucky 40292. If you require assistance or accommodation with our online application process, please contact us by email at  employment@louisville.edu  or by phone 502-852-6258."
