    "https://uofl.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/UofLCareerSite/job/Belknap-Campus/IT-Systems-Engineer-V_R109119": {
      "title": "IT Systems Engineer V",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-10-02\nFull time\n\nDepartment: Location: Belknap Campus Time Type: Full time Worker Type: Regular Job Req ID: R109119 Minimum Requirements: Bachelor's degree in Computer Science or a related field and eight (8) years of relevant experience or an equivalent combination of education and experience. Grade10 (Salaried) Position Description: The University of Louisville is hiring an IT Systems Engineer V reporting to the Associate Vice Provost in ITS Research Computing department. This position is responsible for management and administration of the university’s central research computing infrastructure, including supercomputing clusters, software, and scalable storage systems. This position will work with all aspects of hardware and software systems including assisting with specification, ordering and installation of new hardware, storage and associated networking; installing and administering specialized research software; diagnosing and resolving software and hardware problems; working with hardware and software vendors, and assisting research faculty and users with operation of the systems.   Requires highly experienced individual skilled at managing Linux-based supercomputing clusters, scalable parallel file systems (Lustre preferred) and university research applications.   ESSENTIAL DUTIES AND RESPONSIBILITIES Design, operate and manage highly complex university research supercomputing and storage systems, and develop technical documentation Manage a suite of specialized research software, including Installation and administration of research software licenses and maintenance of OS and system support software Assist with specification, ordering and installation of new hardware, storage and associated networking Perform problem analysis and resolution, monitor and tune performance, configure job schedulers (SLURM) to optimize operation and performance of supercomputing resources Advise and assist faculty, staff and graduate students on effective uses of academic and supercomputing technology for large and complex applications, and assist with development, debugging, and optimization of specialized codes for research Work with faculty to develop research grant proposals to enhance research computing resources and fund extensions to existing centrally-managed systems Consult with users on handling protected data and support the management of the secure enclave on-prem and in Google cloud. Complete special projects and other duties as assigned.   PREFERRED QUALIFICATIONS Experience with Centos/RedHat Enterprise Linux; Linux Virtual Machine and Container Management, Firewalls Scripting in BASH, Python Experience with scientific computing tools, libraries, and frameworks is a plus (e.g., MPI, OpenMP, CUDA, VASP, Gaussian, and Nextflow, Open OnDemand) Experience with the installation, configuration and administration of Docker and Singularity Experience with the installation, configuration and administration of cluster and job/resource management software (e.g., Slurm, Warewulf, DUO) Linux server installation, configuration and administration (OpenHPC, Redhat Enterprise 7/8, CentOS 7/8/Stream, Ubuntu), experience with SELinux is a plus Parallel and distributed file system (Lustre) experience is a plus, as well as knowledge of Ceph Experienced at installing and using databases – Postgres, MySQL, MongoDB Prior experience is configuring NIST 800-171 compliant HPC systems will be a plus Target Compensation Maximum: $130,488.00 Target Compensation Minimum: $86,992.00 Compensation will be commensurate to candidate experience. Equal Employment Opportunity The University of Louisville is an Equal Employment Opportunity employer. The University strives to provide equal employment opportunity on the basis of merit and without unlawful discrimination on the basis of race, sex, age, color, national origin, ethnicity, creed, religion, disability, genetic information, sexual orientation, gender, gender identity or expression, veteran status, marital status, or pregnancy. In accordance with the Rehabilitation Act of 1973 and the Vietnam Era Veteran Readjustment Act of 1974, the University prohibits job discrimination of individuals with disabilities, Vietnam era veterans, qualified special disabled veterans, recently separated veterans, and other protected veterans. The University acknowledges its obligations to ensure affirmative steps are taken to ensure equal employment opportunities for all employees and applicants for employment. It is the policy of the University that no employee or applicant for employment be subject to unlawful discrimination in terms of recruitment, hiring, promotion, contract, contract renewal, tenure, compensation, benefits, and/or working conditions. No employee or applicant for employment is required to endorse or condemn a specific ideology, political viewpoint, or social viewpoint to be eligible for hiring, contract renewal, tenure, or promotion. Consistent with applicable law, demographic information is collected for aggregate reporting requirements. Demographic information provided through this application is not available to hiring managers/committees and is not considered in hiring or employment decisions. Assistance and Accommodations Computers are available for application submission at the Human Resources Department located at 2315 South First Street Walk, Room 02C - Louisville, Kentucky 40292. If you require assistance or accommodation with our online application process, please contact us by email at  employment@louisville.edu  or by phone 502-852-6258."
    },
    "https://uofl.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/UofLCareerSite/job/Belknap-Campus/Professor--Anderson-Endowed-Chair-in-Accountancy_R109474": {
      "title": "Professor, Anderson Endowed Chair in Accountancy",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-10-02\nFull time\n\nDepartment: Accountancy Location: Belknap Campus Time Type: Full time Worker Type: Regular Job Req ID: R109474 Position Description: The School of Accountancy (SOA) in the College of Business at the University of Louisville (UofL) invites applications and nominations for the Anderson Endowed Chair in Accountancy at the rank of Professor with tenure. The Anderson Endowed Chair will be an exceptional scholar-teacher who will contribute to the SOA’s scholarly excellence, educational mission, and national profile. The successful candidate will have the opportunity to help shape the School of Accountancy’s next stage of scholarly and educational development. Working collaboratively with the School Director and faculty, the Anderson Chair will provide intellectual leadership to strengthen the School’s research culture, mentor and develop faculty talent, advance curricular innovation, and expand the School’s national visibility. Key Responsibiliti es Scholarly Leadership: Maintain an active, high-impact research agenda with regular publication in leading accounting journals. Teaching Excellence: Deliver high-quality instruction primarily through face-to-face undergraduate and graduate courses, including courses in the BSBA in Accountancy and Master of Science in Accounting and Analytics (MSAA) programs. The expected teaching load is 9 credit hours per academic year. Faculty Mentorship: Actively mentor junior faculty and support their development toward tenure, promotion, and sustained scholarly productivity. Service: Contribute to curriculum development, assurance of learning processes, college and university committees, and broader academic and professional service. Required Qualifications Earned doctoral degree (Ph.D.) in accounting from an AACSB-accredited institution. Qualifications commensurate with appointment at the rank of Professor with tenure at the time of hire. A sustained and distinguished record of publication in leading accounting and business academic journals. Evidence of an active research agenda with the potential for continued scholarly productivity and impact. Demonstrated excellence in teaching at the undergraduate and/or graduate level. Proven track record of mentoring junior faculty and supporting their professional development toward tenure and promotion. Ability to work collegially and collaboratively across academic and professional communities. Preferred Qualifications A research and teaching specialization in financial accounting, managerial accounting, or taxation is preferred. Exceptional candidates in other accounting areas, including accounting information systems and auditing, will also be considered. Active professional certification (e.g., CPA, CIA, CMA, CFE) and substantial professional accounting experience. Compensation The Anderson Chair is a 10-month, full-time tenured faculty appointment. Compensation is highly competitive and includes a base salary supplemented by a dedicated endowed-chair stipend and research support. Salary determination considers candidate qualifications, scholarly record, internal equity, and market standards. About the School of Accountancy & University of Louisville The College of Business and School of Accountancy are accredited by AACSB International. The SOA offers a BSBA in Accountancy, a Master of Science in Accounting and Analytics (MSAA), an Accounting Minor, and a Certificate in Accounting, enrolling approximately 400 undergraduate majors and 30 graduate students. SOA faculty research appears in premier journals including The Accounting Review, Journal of Accounting Research, Contemporary Accounting Research, AUDITING: A Journal of Practice & Theory, Management Accounting Research, Accounting Horizons, and the Journal of Accounting and Public Policy among other leading scholarly outlets. Student organizations affiliated with the School include Beta Alpha Psi and NABA. Founded in 1798, the University of Louisville is Kentucky’s premier Carnegie R1 metropolitan research university, enrolling over 25,000 students across 12 schools and colleges. UofL’s vision is to be a place where excellence and opportunity converge to drive transformative progress in health and societal well-being. Its location in Louisville provides distinctive opportunities for research, experiential learning, community engagement, and partnerships with business, government, nonprofit, health care, and other organizations. As a metropolitan research university, UofL supports student success by expanding access, creating opportunity and fostering an environment in which students can thrive. Faculty benefit from the resources of a major research university while working within a community that values collaboration, innovation, engaged scholarship, and meaningful impact. The University follows the C.A.R.D.I.N.A.L. Principles—Community of Care, Agility, Respect, Diversity, Integrity and Transparency, Noble Purpose, Accountability, and Leadership—which guide the University community and its work. To Apply Applicants should submit a single PDF containing: Cover letter highlighting strategic fit, research agenda, and interest in the position; Curriculum vitae; Research statement describing the candidate’s scholarly contributions, identifying representative publications, and works in progress; Evidence of teaching effectiveness; and Names and contact information for three professional references. Review of applications will begin November 15, 2026, and continue until the position is filled. Direct inquiries, nominations, or confidential questions to Dr. Dereck Barr-Pulliam, Director of the School of Accountancy, at d0barr10@louisville.edu. Equal Employment Opportunity The University of Louisville is an Equal Employment Opportunity employer. The University strives to provide equal employment opportunity on the basis of merit and without unlawful discrimination on the basis of race, sex, age, color, national origin, ethnicity, creed, religion, disability, genetic information, sexual orientation, gender, gender identity or expression, veteran status, marital status, or pregnancy. In accordance with the Rehabilitation Act of 1973 and the Vietnam Era Veteran Readjustment Act of 1974, the University prohibits job discrimination of individuals with disabilities, Vietnam era veterans, qualified special disabled veterans, recently separated veterans, and other protected veterans. The University acknowledges its obligations to ensure affirmative steps are taken to ensure equal employment opportunities for all employees and applicants for employment. It is the policy of the University that no employee or applicant for employment be subject to unlawful discrimination in terms of recruitment, hiring, promotion, contract, contract renewal, tenure, compensation, benefits, and/or working conditions. No employee or applicant for employment is required to endorse or condemn a specific ideology, political viewpoint, or social viewpoint to be eligible for hiring, contract renewal, tenure, or promotion. Consistent with applicable law, demographic information is collected for aggregate reporting requirements. Demographic information provided through this application is not available to hiring managers/committees and is not considered in hiring or employment decisions. Assistance and Accommodations Computers are available for application submission at the Human Resources Department located at 2315 South First Street Walk, Room 02C - Louisville, Kentucky 40292. If you require assistance or accommodation with our online application process, please contact us by email at  employment@louisville.edu  or by phone 502-852-6258."
    },
    "https://uofl.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/UofLCareerSite/job/Health-Sciences-Center/Professor--Open-Rank-_R109457": {
      "title": "Professor (Open Rank)",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-10-02\nFull time\n\nDepartment: Oral Immun & Infect Dis Location: Health Sciences Center Time Type: Full time Worker Type: Regular Job Req ID: R109457 Position Description: The Department of Oral Immunology and Infectious Diseases at the University of Louisville (https://dentistry.louisville.edu/about/leadership-organization/departments/oral-immunology-infectious-diseases) invites applications for a faculty position at the Assistant, Associate or Full Professor level.   The department comprises investigators focused on microbial pathogenicity, innate immunity and inflammation, and we are looking for applicants interested in fundamental disease mechanisms and host responses.  Academic rank and salary will be commensurate with qualifications and experience. The successful candidate at the Associate or Full Professor level will have an outstanding record of innovative, well-funded research and will be expected to maintain a strong extramurally funded research program.  At the Assistant professor level, the successful candidate will have a strong publication record and the potential to obtain independent funding.  The selected individual will have primary responsibilities in maintaining or establishing an extramurally funded research program in oral microbiology or immunology and contribute to the teaching of graduate students and residents. Minimum Qualifications PhD, DDS/DMD, MD or related terminal degree Experience in oral microbiology or immunology research Qualified applicants should submit a letter of interest (including date of availability), curriculum vitae, and two professional reference letters with their application. Equal Employment Opportunity The University of Louisville is an Equal Employment Opportunity employer. The University strives to provide equal employment opportunity on the basis of merit and without unlawful discrimination on the basis of race, sex, age, color, national origin, ethnicity, creed, religion, disability, genetic information, sexual orientation, gender, gender identity or expression, veteran status, marital status, or pregnancy. In accordance with the Rehabilitation Act of 1973 and the Vietnam Era Veteran Readjustment Act of 1974, the University prohibits job discrimination of individuals with disabilities, Vietnam era veterans, qualified special disabled veterans, recently separated veterans, and other protected veterans. The University acknowledges its obligations to ensure affirmative steps are taken to ensure equal employment opportunities for all employees and applicants for employment. It is the policy of the University that no employee or applicant for employment be subject to unlawful discrimination in terms of recruitment, hiring, promotion, contract, contract renewal, tenure, compensation, benefits, and/or working conditions. No employee or applicant for employment is required to endorse or condemn a specific ideology, political viewpoint, or social viewpoint to be eligible for hiring, contract renewal, tenure, or promotion. Consistent with applicable law, demographic information is collected for aggregate reporting requirements. Demographic information provided through this application is not available to hiring managers/committees and is not considered in hiring or employment decisions. Assistance and Accommodations Computers are available for application submission at the Human Resources Department located at 2315 South First Street Walk, Room 02C - Louisville, Kentucky 40292. If you require assistance or accommodation with our online application process, please contact us by email at  employment@louisville.edu  or by phone 502-852-6258."
