    "https://uofl.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/UofLCareerSite/job/Belknap-Campus/Department-Coordinator-IV_R109248-2": {
      "title": "Department Coordinator IV",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-10-16\nFull time\n\nDepartment: Location: Belknap Campus Time Type: Full time Worker Type: Regular Job Req ID: R109248 Minimum Requirements: Bachelor's degree in a related field or an equivalent combination of education and experience. Grade 4 (Hourly) Position Description: The PEACC Center in the Division of Student Affairs is seeking to hire a Department Coordinator IV. The Department Coordinator provides support, advocacy to connect with resources, and referrals to students impacted by power-based personal violence in the University community. The Department Coordinator works to end power-based violence through prevention strategies and initiatives, education and outreach, and by assisting and providing advocacy to connect to resources for students affected by sexual assault, dating violence, stalking, or sexual harassment. The Department Coordinator serves as the lead intake clinician and advocate providing support to students impacted by power-based violence, ensuring all services provided are confidential and personalized with the goal of providing students with information regarding their options and keeping their safety and wellbeing as the focus. Essential Duties and Responsibilities Provide personalized support and advocacy to connect with resources and services for students by providing referrals to therapeutic services and legal services both on campus and in the community at large Assist the Senior Manager with the design, coordination and presentation of prevention educational programming related to power-based violence and services and resources for the campus community Assist in the development and implementation of outreach and projects designed for University functions such as Domestic Violence Awareness Month, Sexual Assault Awareness Month, etc. Train, engage and supervise student staff and volunteers and graduate practicum trainee students assigned to the PEACC Center Leverage social media and creates marketing materials to increase campus and student awareness Provide outreach and programming for personal violence prevention and consultation to referring faculty and staff Assist in coordination of program research, data collection and annual reporting as required by government regulations Attend training and conferences for professional development as applicable Serve as the PEACC Center representative on campus and community committees and develop collaborative partnerships to strengthen prevention initiatives throughout the campus community Support The PEACC Center’s daily operations including performing administrative tasks and managing crisis/emergency needs Assist with other Division initiatives and operations such as freshman orientation, Welcome Week, and resident move-in events Perform other duties and complete special projects as assigned Preferred Qualifications Master's degree in counseling, social work, public health, psychology, sociology,  or a related field Two (2) years of professional experience related to prevention and advocacy to connect clients to resources and services 40-hour Victim Advocacy training certification Sexual harassment prevention training certification Experience designing and implementing successful outreach programs Experience effectively working with student populations Experience participating on committees for coalition-building Experience working constructively and with a positive attitude in an institutional setting Marketing and/or graphic design experience Work Hours and Location This is a full-time, non-exempt position with a 37.5-hour regular workweek including evening and weekend hours as required. Additional work hours may be required based on department needs, programming, staffing, and/or scheduling. The primary work location for this position is at the Belknap Campus. Occasional overnight travel (for professional development and conferences) may be required. Employee Benefits The University of Louisville offers competitive benefits and perks to eligible full-time and part-time employees. Please visit the University of Louisville Total Rewards webpages for details. Student Affairs The Division of Student Affairs encourages candidates to apply who have demonstrated the capacity to create supportive work environments, collaborate effectively on teams, and serve the students we admit and aim to attract. Please submit a resume, cover letter, and three professional references with your application Compensation will be commensurate to candidate experience. Equal Employment Opportunity The University of Louisville is an Equal Employment Opportunity employer. The University strives to provide equal employment opportunity on the basis of merit and without unlawful discrimination on the basis of race, sex, age, color, national origin, ethnicity, creed, religion, disability, genetic information, sexual orientation, gender, gender identity or expression, veteran status, marital status, or pregnancy. In accordance with the Rehabilitation Act of 1973 and the Vietnam Era Veteran Readjustment Act of 1974, the University prohibits job discrimination of individuals with disabilities, Vietnam era veterans, qualified special disabled veterans, recently separated veterans, and other protected veterans. The University acknowledges its obligations to ensure affirmative steps are taken to ensure equal employment opportunities for all employees and applicants for employment. It is the policy of the University that no employee or applicant for employment be subject to unlawful discrimination in terms of recruitment, hiring, promotion, contract, contract renewal, tenure, compensation, benefits, and/or working conditions. No employee or applicant for employment is required to endorse or condemn a specific ideology, political viewpoint, or social viewpoint to be eligible for hiring, contract renewal, tenure, or promotion. Consistent with applicable law, demographic information is collected for aggregate reporting requirements. Demographic information provided through this application is not available to hiring managers/committees and is not considered in hiring or employment decisions. Assistance and Accommodations Computers are available for application submission at the Human Resources Department located at 2315 South First Street Walk, Room 02C - Louisville, Kentucky 40292. If you require assistance or accommodation with our online application process, please contact us by email at  employment@louisville.edu  or by phone 502-852-6258."
    },
    "https://uofl.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/UofLCareerSite/job/Belknap-Campus/Director--Annual-Giving_R109453": {
      "title": "Director, Annual Giving",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-10-16\nFull time\n\nDepartment: Location: Belknap Campus Time Type: Full time Worker Type: Regular Job Req ID: R109453 Minimum Requirements: Bachelor's degree in a related field and six (6) years of relevant experience. Related work experience may include: Major giving fund-raising, public relations, communications, and/or management experience, especially in a higher education setting or the equivalent combination of education and experience. Grade 9 (Salaried) Position Description: The Director of Annual Giving provides strategic leadership for the university's early-stage donor development efforts and is responsible for building a robust pipeline of future major gift donors. The position oversees annual giving efforts, including leadership annual giving programs, while managing the Associate Director of Annual Giving and the Assistant Director of Leadership Annual Giving. Through data-driven strategies, pipeline management, and team leadership, the Director advances donors through the philanthropic continuum and ensures a sustainable flow of qualified prospects into the major gift program. Duties & Responsibilities   Donor Pipeline Strategy and Management   Develop and implement a proactive multi-year strategy for identifying and cultivating emerging major gift prospects. Create a portfolio of donors and prospects with the capacity and inclination to make leadership annual gifts between $25,000-49,999. Identify and transition donors capable of making future major gifts into philanthropy officer portfolios. Design qualification and engagement pathways that move individuals through the donor continuum. Establish metrics and benchmarks to evaluate pipeline growth, conversion rates, and donor advancement.   Leadership and Supervision   Directly supervise the Associate Director of Annual Giving and the Assistant Director of Annual Giving – Leadership Annual Giving. Provide strategic leadership, coaching, performance management, and professional development for direct reports. Establish annual goals, performance metrics, and accountability measures aligned with institutional fundraising priorities. Foster collaboration among annual giving, alumni engagement, major gifts, advancement services, and constituent experience teams. Ensure coordinated donor engagement strategies that effectively transition donors from annual giving through leadership annual giving and into major gift portfolios. Lead regular portfolio reviews and prospect strategy discussions to optimize donor movement throughout the philanthropic pipeline. Promote a culture of innovation, collaboration, inclusivity, and results-oriented fundraising.     Team and Program Leadership Lead the university's early pipeline fundraising strategy, including leadership annual giving. Develop annual operating plans and fundraising objectives for supervised staff and programs. Oversee donor segmentation, prospect qualification efforts, and leadership annual giving strategies. Monitor performance metrics related to donor acquisition, retention, leadership annual giving growth, qualified prospect generation, and major gift pipeline development. Partner with development officers, deans, and advancement leaders to align pipeline-building activities with school, college, and university fundraising priorities.   Collaboration with Advancement Teams   Partner with, Alumni Engagement, Constituent Experience, and Philanthropy Officers to identify and advance prospective donors. Collaborate with PAE Operations and Prospect Development teams to leverage data-driven prospect identification strategies. Work closely with schools, colleges, and units to align donor interests with institutional priorities. Facilitate smooth transitions of qualified prospects to major gift officers.   Data Analysis and Reporting   Monitor pipeline performance and donor engagement metrics. Prepare regular reports on prospect movement, qualification rates, fundraising outcomes, and portfolio performance. Utilize data analytics to inform strategic decision-making and resource allocation. Recommend process improvements that enhance donor pipeline efficiency and effectiveness.   Occasional travel and weekend work may be required. *Please include a cover letter along with your resume   Philanthropy & Alumni Engagement is committed to creating campus environments that support the success of all students and employees. We believe that employees who feel valued and respected will create policies, programs, practices, research agendas, and services that effectively meet the needs and exceed the expectations of our student population.   Benefits - The University of Louisville has an exceptional benefits package; to learn more about our benefits, please visit  HERE   Preferred Qualifications Master's degree. Experience in higher education fundraising or nonprofit development. Knowledge of major gift fundraising principles and donor lifecycle management. Experience qualifying prospects and advancing donors through a philanthropic pipeline. Familiarity with campaign planning and fundraising analytics. Strategic relationship-building and networking skills. Strong project and portfolio management abilities. Data-informed decision making. Excellent presentation and communication skills. Ability to work independently and collaboratively across teams. Sound judgment and discretion in handling confidential information. Commitment to diversity, inclusion, and constituent-centered engagement.   Competencies   Demonstrated success in building relationships and managing constituent portfolios. Strong written, verbal, and interpersonal communication skills. Experience using CRM systems and donor databases. Ability to analyze data and translate findings into actionable strategies.   Compensation will be commensurate to candidate experience. Equal Employment Opportunity The University of Louisville is an Equal Employment Opportunity employer. The University strives to provide equal employment opportunity on the basis of merit and without unlawful discrimination on the basis of race, sex, age, color, national origin, ethnicity, creed, religion, disability, genetic information, sexual orientation, gender, gender identity or expression, veteran status, marital status, or pregnancy. In accordance with the Rehabilitation Act of 1973 and the Vietnam Era Veteran Readjustment Act of 1974, the University prohibits job discrimination of individuals with disabilities, Vietnam era veterans, qualified special disabled veterans, recently separated veterans, and other protected veterans. The University acknowledges its obligations to ensure affirmative steps are taken to ensure equal employment opportunities for all employees and applicants for employment. It is the policy of the University that no employee or applicant for employment be subject to unlawful discrimination in terms of recruitment, hiring, promotion, contract, contract renewal, tenure, compensation, benefits, and/or working conditions. No employee or applicant for employment is required to endorse or condemn a specific ideology, political viewpoint, or social viewpoint to be eligible for hiring, contract renewal, tenure, or promotion. Consistent with applicable law, demographic information is collected for aggregate reporting requirements. Demographic information provided through this application is not available to hiring managers/committees and is not considered in hiring or employment decisions. Assistance and Accommodations Computers are available for application submission at the Human Resources Department located at 2315 South First Street Walk, Room 02C - Louisville, Kentucky 40292. If you require assistance or accommodation with our online application process, please contact us by email at  employment@louisville.edu  or by phone 502-852-6258."
